    {
      "id": "p0165-b008",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In Japanese, urameshiya (roughly \"oh, how I begn1dge you\" or \"I curse you\") is a stereotypical phrase that ghosts say, similar to how ghosts say \"boo\" or \"oo\" in some English speaking regions. Even with these new Japanese lyrics, however, the song was crafted to follow the tune of \"Jingle Bells\".",
        "raw": "In Japanese, urameshiya (roughly\n\"oh, how I begn1dge you\" or \"I curse\nyou\") is a stereotypical phrase that\nghosts say, similar to how ghosts\nsay \"boo\" or \"oo\" in some English\nspeaking regions. Even with these\nnew Japanese lyrics, however, the\nsong was crafted to follow the tune of\n\"Jingle Bells\".",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.08604971,
          0.66135714,
          0.38388978,
          0.82161429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.0847243,
          0.66064286,
          0.38521519,
          0.82268571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          47,
          50,
          52,
          54,
          56,
          58,
          60,
          64,
          66
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "日语中的 urameshiya，大意为“我好恨你啊”或“我诅咒你”，是鬼魂常说的固定台词，类似一些英语地区的“boo”或“oo”。即使用了这套新日文歌词，歌曲仍然配合《铃儿响叮当》的旋律。",
        "status": "draft",
